<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 685–705</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan was an Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> who reigned from 685 to 705. He reunited the caliphate after a period of civil war, made Arabic the language of administration and introduced a distinctively Islamic gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage. He also commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and his reforms gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> much of its lasting governmental structure.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan.jpg" alt="Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Umayyad_gold_dinar,_Caliph_Abd_al-Malik,_695_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
